#!/usr/bin/env python3
# =============================================================================
# h96-restore - replay an h96-backup archive onto a freshly reflashed H96 Max V58.
#
# Reflashing wipes the eMMC back to the console base. h96-restore reads the
# archive's manifest.json, re-runs the on-demand installers for the features
# that were present, then restores the passive data (configs, WiFi, Waydroid
# /data, emulator saves, wine prefix) to the right paths and owners.
#
#   h96-restore                        list the archives found and ask which one
#   h96-restore <archive>              auto: run each installer, then restore data
#   h96-restore <folder>               use the newest archive in that folder
#   h96-restore --list                 print the archives found, change nothing
#   h96-restore <archive> --open       do NOT auto-run: print/launch the installer
#                                       commands for you to confirm, then restore
#                                       the passive data (configs/wifi/saves)
#   h96-restore <archive> --dry-run    print the plan only, change nothing
#   h96-restore <archive> --force      overwrite files that are newer on disk
#   h96-restore <archive> --only a,b   restore only these categories
#   h96-restore <archive> --skip a,b   restore everything except these categories
#   h96-restore <archive> --emulators x,y   limit which emulators are reinstalled
#   h96-restore <archive> --plan-json  print what the archive holds as JSON (no root)
#
# Categories: desktop configs emulators waydroid waydroid-images widevine gaming
# npu wifi autologin system vm. h96-backup-gui is the graphical front end.
#
# Idempotent and safe: a config file already present and NEWER than the archived
# copy is kept (and noted), not clobbered, unless --force is given. On a fresh
# image a desktop session writes default configs before the restore runs, so
# those defaults count as newer; --force (with the session stopped) is the way
# to get the archived desktop configuration back in that case. WiFi confs
# restore as root:root 0600.
# User files restore owned by the DYNAMICALLY detected desktop user. Any feature
# whose installer is missing is skipped cleanly. Auto-elevates via sudo.
#
# Reads an archive made on v5.0.1 or any later release (schema 1 or 2); a
# schema-1 archive simply has no display/services/wol/vm state, so those steps
# are skipped. Opt-in features (h96-leds, h96-cec, WoL) are only ever turned ON
# when the archive recorded them on; a feature this box never had is never
# enabled just because the archive is missing the field. Pinhole loader mode
# does not ship (tested, doesn't work); an older archive that recorded it on
# is noted and skipped, never fails.
# h96-edid-adapt is retired on v6.2: its service/udev rule are never restored,
# from any archive. /boot is never touched by this tool, on any archive.
# Autologin never restores the archived lightdm.conf/lightdm.conf.d files
# (distro- and h96-image-owned; an old copy can regress a newer install) --
# only the autologin-user (and autologin-session/user-session) setting is
# read out of them and applied through one h96-owned drop-in.
# =============================================================================
